# Normalise line endings.
#
# The repository stores LF. Without this, Git for Windows checks files out with
# CRLF, so a contributor on Windows and one on Linux see every line as modified
# even when nothing changed, and CI diffs become unreadable.
#
# `text=auto` lets Git detect text files and store them with LF, while
# `eol=lf` forces LF in the working tree as well. This project is developed on
# Windows and tested on Linux and macOS, so a single convention is worth having.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF if they are ever executed by cmd.exe or by
# a PowerShell version that is strict about line endings. These are the only
# exceptions; everything else stays LF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.docx binary
*.xlsx binary
*.pdf binary
*.db binary
*.pyc binary

# Keep generated artifacts out of language statistics (they are git-ignored, but
# this also stops `git diff --stat` noise if they are ever force-added).
data/applications.jsonl -diff
reports/*.json -diff
